PANVK_REPO=ryanistr/DroidDeck-Mali
PANVK_TAG=panvk-linux-g57-1.2.0-p4
PANVK_SHA256=dc6503e3e9aa649387e01022c6337276c6e150ca4093b05c07f3226fef97fd88
# Built from the Mali-G57 fork (tools/panvk-g57/build-panvk-g57.sh + its patches) rather than from
# our own mesa-patches: only that tree has a Valhall JM backend at all, and from v1.2.0 it gates the
# external memory/semaphore fd group on PANVK_FD_INTEROP, which gamescope requires. p13 shipped no
# v9 backend, so a Valhall device enumerated no GPU and gamescope reported no physical device.
# p4 additionally carries 0003, which ports Valhall gen3 (arch 11, Mali-G615/G715) back into the
# fork; without it a GT 30 Pro has the same "no physical device" failure as p13 had on a G77.
#
# The driver names itself after its tag from p5 on: PanVK-rynx-<version>-<patch>, e.g.
# PanVK-rynx-1.2.0-p5. That code name is what tells this fork's build apart from any other PanVK
# package in the driver lists, the device report and a bug report; it is written by the build
# script and read back by LinuxPanvkDriverManager.getBuiltinName().
#
# p5 is the CSF build and is NOT published yet: it is p4 plus 0004 (the CSF provoking-vertex
# address truncation), 0005 (kbase reports node rdev as a DRM device), 0006 (single merged CSG
# with fence-wait reclaim) and 0007 (the CS register file sized from the kbase uAPI instead of a
# fixed 96, plus the missing nr_kernel_registers on the job-emission builder; that pair is
# wonderkast02/panvk-g720-kbase-csf 0.1.0-beta.2's fix, arrived at from the other end). It
# declares gpuArch [9, 10, 11]. Publish it with
#   gh workflow run build-panvk-g57.yml --ref <branch> -f version=1.2.0 -f tag=panvk-linux-g57-1.2.0-p5
# then set PANVK_TAG/PANVK_SHA256 here to the new asset and re-run tools/build_local.sh.
PANVK_SOURCE="mesa-panvk-mali-g57 v1.2.0"
PANVK_WORKFLOW=.github/workflows/build-panvk-g57.yml